Large language models can favor familiar statements even when the supplied premises do not support them.

_Contextual calibration_ reduces such answer preferences in tasks like multiple choice tests, where models produce short answers without an explicit reasoning trace. We adapt this idea to multi-step reasoning. Our _context relevance_ metric compares a step's average token log likelihood with and without its question or preceding steps.

We evaluate context relevance as a first-error detection signal on three datasets (MR-GSM8K, BIG-Bench Mistake, and ProcessBench) containing annotated solutions to mathematical and logical questions. We compute the per-step context relevance for 8,584 reasoning traces using five language models from the Qwen and Llama families. Pooling steps within each dataset, fully ablated context relevance is associated with reasoning errors across all five models (AUROC 0.645–0.751 for negative context relevance). On tracking shuffled objects, question-ablated context relevance reaches an AUROC of 0.904.

As a baseline, we evaluate the models' ability to find reasoning errors when explicitly prompted to do so (_LLM judge_). Qwen3.5-27B is the strongest judge we evaluated. Its reported scores exceed some published GPT-4 results, although differences in prompts and evaluation protocols limit direct comparison.

Despite the association between low context relevance and reasoning errors, the stronger LLM judges locate errors more accurately than the context relevance signal alone. Across the three benchmarks, Qwen3.5-27B achieves 43.4–72.1% exact first-error-or-NONE accuracy as a judge, compared with 21.9–47.7% for a classifier based on its fully ablated context relevance. The classifier's thresholds are optimized and evaluated on the same traces. Even these optimistic in-sample accuracies barely exceed an always-NONE baseline.
